On Apr 8, 7:11 am, ran...@pactechdata.com wrote:
> On Apr 7, 1:43 pm, "guns" <sungmust...@gmail.com> wrote:

> > Diff utility? [...]
> > What is the technique? Do you make a copy of of the source file, make
> > your changes, and then run the diff command? That would solve all the
> > little whitespace issues I guess.

>      The recommended technique is to retain a full, unmodified
> copy of the sources in one directory (and associated sub-dirs)
> and work on your modifications with a full copy in another dir.
> Then from a directory above the ones involved, use
>   diff -c original_dir/original.file working_dir/modified.file >
> output.file
> You can use `diff -u' instead of `diff -c'; either way, you
> want the old file first and revised file second, otherwise the
> diff comes out backwards from what patch expects.

>      Alternatively, maintain a single directory tree and copy
> each file you plan to modify, using some naming scheme (such
> as appending "-original" to the file name or type), prior to
> making the first change to it.  Then the diff-ing process
> would be similar except that you (usually) stay within same
> directory instead of moving to a level above.
>   diff -c some.file-original some.file > output.file

>      The first method has at least two advantages:  (1) it's
> a lot easier to revert to the unmodified files, by simply
> copying the originals into your working directory, and (2)
> some versions of diff can process whole directories at once,
> going through one pair of corresponding files after another.
> That won't work with the second method.  Oh, and (3) you
> won't forget to save the original when you go off an modify
> some particular file for the first time.

>      The second method has the advantage that you can tell
> at a glance which files you're working on, by the presence
> of their copies (provided that you only copy the ones which
> are being changed, and that you don't forget to make those
> copies...).

Just a nit, but...  My editor (and every editor I've used for
the last umpteen years) makes back-up files with predetermined
names.  To find out which files I may have used: ls *.orig (or
ls *#*, if I'm using emace).

